00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:17,040
When you get tumors removed and they give you all that data about them,

00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:22,040
is there any of it important to know for treatment moving forward?

00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:25,040
Yes, of course there is.

00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:31,040
So they should do what's called a pathology report on that biopsy.

00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:35,040
So if they removed the tumor or they did a biopsy of a tumor,

00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:41,040
they're doing a pathology report to see what that cell type of that tumor is.

00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:49,040
If it is cancer, so you can have a tumor removed and it's benign, it's not cancerous,

00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:54,040
that you're going to get that report, it's not cancerous, and that was the pathology report.

00:00:54,040 --> 00:01:03,040
If you get a tumor removed and it was cancerous, then they will be testing for what type of cancer it is.

00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:08,040
More than likely it's going to be a metastasis of the primary cancer.

00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:15,040
We have people maybe on this call today that have several different primary cancers.

00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:20,040
That's fairly rare, but it is possible person can have liver cancer and breast cancer.

00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:28,040
So that's why if they do a biopsy of let's say deliver and of a person who has no breast cancer,

00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:32,040
you don't just assume that it's breast cancer metastasized to the liver.

00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,040
It could be liver cancer.

00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:37,040
First of all, they have to find out if it's cancer and they'll see what cell type it is.

00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:42,040
And let's say if it's breast cancer, then it's breast cancer metastasized to the liver.

00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:46,040
So would there be change a change in the right program?

00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:50,040
Probably not, would there be a change in maybe supplement?

